window.PH_BOOK_LOAD({"id":"16-05","translated":true,"translator":"AI (Claude)","sections":[{"slug":"16-05-01","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/16-05-01/","title_he":"א – זמן קיום מצוות השביעית והיובל","title_en":"1. When the Mitzvot of Shevi'it and Yovel Apply","body_en":"<p>It is a Torah mitzva that Israel enter the Land, divide the Land among the tribes, divide the inheritance of each tribe among families and fathers' houses, work their fields for six years to grow their fruit, and in the seventh year rest from the work of the field and release the fruit and the debts. And so they shall do for seven cycles, in which they will keep seven shemitot, forty-nine years in all. After that they shall sanctify the fiftieth year and make it a Yovel (Jubilee), in which they will free the slaves to their homes and return the fields to their owners. As it is said (Vayikra 25:2-10): \"When you come into the land which I give you, then shall the land keep a Sabbath to the Lord. Six years you shall sow your field, and six years you shall prune your vineyard, and gather in its produce. But in the seventh year shall be a Sabbath of solemn rest for the land, a Sabbath to the Lord… And you shall number seven Sabbaths of years for yourself, seven times seven years; and there shall be to you the days of seven Sabbaths of years, forty-nine years… And you shall hallow the fiftieth year, and proclaim liberty throughout the land to all its inhabitants; it shall be a jubilee to you, and you shall return every man to his possession, and you shall return every man to his family.\"</p>\n<p>And indeed with the mitzva of separating ḥalla Israel were commanded immediately after they crossed the Jordan and entered the main part of the Land; but with the mitzvot of Shevi'it and Yovel they were commanded only after they had finished dividing the Land among all Israel. As it is said regarding Shevi'it (ibid. 3): \"Six years you shall sow your field, and six years you shall prune your vineyard, and gather in its produce.\" The Sages expounded (Sifra there): \"'Your field' – that each and every one recognise his field; 'your vineyard' – that each and every one recognise his vineyard.\" And likewise it is said regarding the Yovel (Vayikra 25:10): \"And proclaim liberty throughout the land to all its inhabitants\"; the Sages expounded (Arakhin 32b): 'to all its inhabitants' – \"at a time when its inhabitants are in their proper places, and not at a time when they are mixed,\" so that even if all were living in their Land but the tribes were mixed with one another, Shevi'it and Yovel do not apply. Therefore they had to wait until they finished conquering the Land and dividing it into inheritances.</p>\n<p>In the year 2,489 by the count we use from the creation of the world, Israel entered the Land under the leadership of Yehoshua bin Nun. For seven years they were occupied in conquering the Land and for seven years in dividing it among the tribes and families (Arakhin 12b). After they finished dividing the Land and every person of Israel knew where his inheritance was, they became obligated in the mitzvot of Shevi'it and Yovel and began to count the years. It follows that in the fifteenth year of their entry into the Land, which is the year 2,504, they began to count the years; in the 21st year of their entry into the Land they kept the first Shevi'it; and in the 64th year of their entry into the Land they kept the first Yovel (Tosefta Menaḥot 6:20; Rambam 10:2).</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"16-05-02","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/16-05-02/","title_he":"ב – גבולות עולי מצרים ודין סוריה","title_en":"2. The Borders of Those Who Came Up from Egypt, and the Law of Syria","body_en":"<p>The borders of the Land that the Lord gave to Israel are wide, from the river of Egypt to the river Euphrates, as it is said (Bereshit 15:18): \"On that day the Lord made a covenant with Avram, saying: To your seed have I given this land, from the river of Egypt to the great river, the river Euphrates.\" And likewise we were commanded (Devarim 1:7-8): \"Turn, and take your journey, and go to the hill country of the Amorites and to all the places near it, in the Arava, in the hill country, and in the lowland, and in the Negev, and by the seashore, the land of the Canaanites, and Lebanon, as far as the great river, the river Euphrates. Behold, I have set the land before you; go in and possess the land which the Lord swore to your fathers, to Avraham, to Yitzḥak and to Ya'akov, to give to them and to their seed after them.\" And likewise it is said (11:24): \"Every place on which the sole of your foot shall tread shall be yours; from the wilderness and Lebanon, from the river, the river Euphrates, to the western sea shall be your border.\"</p>\n<p>And the Lord commanded Israel to conquer first the main part of the Land, that is, the western side of the Jordan within the borders stated in parashat Mas'ei (Bamidbar 34), and after they had settled their inheritances properly, to continue to expand until they had settled all the borders of the Land. And since Siḥon and Og opened war against Israel, Israel conquered their land, and so large parts of the eastern side of the Jordan fell into Israel's hands; and the people of the tribes of Re'uven and Gad asked that their inheritance be on the eastern side of the Jordan, and Moshe granted their request (Peninei Halakha, The Nation and the Land 3:18).</p>\n<p>And although the Land of Israel in all its borders is holy, the obligation of the mitzvot dependent on the Land applies only to the areas conquered by Moshe and Yehoshua bin Nun and divided among the tribes. And these borders are called the borders of 'those who came up from Egypt'. And although within the inheritances of the tribes that Yehoshua divided there remained enclaves of gentiles that had not yet been conquered in the days of Yehoshua, since they were divided among the tribes, after they were conquered the holiness of the Land applied to them and they became obligated in the mitzvot dependent on it (Rambam, Terumot 1:2).</p>\n<p>And the halakha is that any additional place that Israel conquer in the Land of Israel in the name of the majority of Israel, under the leadership of a king or a prophet, becomes sanctified, and they become obligated in it in all the mitzvot dependent on the Land. But if one tribe conquers an additional inheritance in the Land, that place does not become sanctified for the mitzvot of the Land. And King David conquered Syria (II Shmuel 8:3-13); and although he was the king of Israel and acted according to the great beit din, the conquest of Syria is considered an individual's conquest, because the mitzva was that Israel first conquer the main part of the Land west of the Jordan, and especially Jerusalem. And since he did not first conquer Jerusalem, Syria did not become sanctified as the Land of Israel; rather its status is an intermediate status, and only rabbinically is one obliged to keep in it the mitzvot dependent on the Land (Gittin 8a-b; Avoda Zara 20b-21a; Rambam, Terumot 1:2-4).<sup>1</sup> And although because of the pressure the Sages could have exempted Syria from the prohibition of work in Shevi'it, they did not want to do so, so that Israel would not go and settle there and leave the main part of the Land (Yerushalmi Shevi'it 6:2; and Rambam, Shevi'it 4:27).</p>","notes_en":[{"n":1,"en":"The basis of the Rambam's words regarding the status of Syria is in the Sifrei, Ekev: \"David acted not according to the Torah. The Torah said: once you have conquered the Land of Israel you will be permitted to conquer outside the Land. And he did not do so, but went and conquered Aram Naharayim and Aram Tzova, and the Jebusite near Jerusalem he did not drive out. The Omnipresent said to him: The Jebusite near your palace you did not drive out, how do you go and conquer Aram Naharayim and Aram Tzova?!\" And so wrote Tosafot, Gittin 8a, s.v. 'Kibbush yaḥid'. And Rashi there explained that his conquest is considered an individual's conquest because not all Israel were with him and he conquered for his own needs."}]},{"slug":"16-05-03","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/16-05-03/","title_he":"ג – ימי חורבן בית המקדש הראשון","title_en":"3. The Days of the Destruction of the First Temple","body_en":"<p>Because of our many sins, sin grew strong; many went after idolatry and did not keep the mitzvot of Shevi'it and Yovel properly, until the curse stated in the Torah began to be fulfilled. The kings of the nations began to prevail over Israel, until in the year 3,188 from the creation of the world Tiglat-Pileser, king of Assyria, exiled the tribes of Re'uven and Gad and half the tribe of Menashe from their inheritance on the eastern side of the Jordan. Presumably many of the people of those tribes fled to the western side of the Jordan, but they no longer had land. By this the balance between all the tribes of Israel was broken, poverty grew, and it was no longer possible to keep the mitzva of Shevi'it properly. And likewise we have learned (in halakha 1) that only when all the tribes are living on their land, each tribe in its place and not mixed with one another, is one obliged by Torah law to keep the mitzva of Shevi'it, as it is said (Vayikra 25:10): \"And proclaim liberty throughout the land to all its inhabitants\" – \"at a time when its inhabitants are in their proper places, and not at a time when they are mixed\" (Arakhin 32b); and the halakha is that the law of Shevi'it depends on the law of Yovel, and when the mitzva of Yovel was annulled by Torah law, the mitzva of Shevi'it too was annulled by Torah law (Gittin 36a).</p>\n<p>And even so, despite the pressure, Israel, with the agreement of the sages of the generation, continued to keep Shevi'it and Yovel, in the hope that soon the exiles would return to their place and Yovel and Shevi'it would again apply by Torah law. But the sins grew, and within about twenty years the kingdom of Assyria exiled also the tribes that were partners in the kingdom of Israel. And about a hundred and thirty years later the sins grew further, and the Temple was destroyed, and even the tribes of Yehuda, Binyamin and Levi were exiled from the Land. Then the curse stated in the Torah was fulfilled (Vayikra 26:32-35): \"And I will bring the land into desolation, and your enemies who dwell in it shall be astonished at it. And I will scatter you among the nations, and will draw out the sword after you; and your land shall be a desolation, and your cities shall be waste. Then shall the land be paid her Sabbaths, as long as it lies desolate, and you are in your enemies' land; even then shall the land rest, and repay her Sabbaths. As long as it lies desolate it shall have rest, the rest which it did not have in your Sabbaths, when you dwelt upon it.\"</p>\n<p>It follows that for close to seven hundred years all the tribes of Israel lived in their inheritance, and the obligation of Shevi'it and Yovel was from the Torah. And from the time the tribes of Re'uven and Gad and half the tribe of Menashe were exiled from their inheritance on the eastern side of the Jordan, in the year 3,188 from the creation of the world, the mitzva of Yovel was annulled by Torah law. But Israel continued to keep Shevi'it and Yovel rabbinically for another 151 years. And in the year 3,339 from the creation of the world, at the end of the shemita year, the First Temple was destroyed, and Israel stopped counting Yovelot, and the obligation to keep the mitzva of Shevi'it was annulled. For the halakha is that the first sanctification, with which Israel sanctified the Land for its mitzvot, was annulled with the destruction of the Temple. Thus in all Israel kept seventeen Yovelot, and the seventeenth Yovel they did not complete, for in the thirty-sixth year of the Yovel the First Temple was destroyed (Arakhin 12b; 32b; Rambam 10:3).<sup>2</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":2,"en":"In the Gemara, Arakhin 32b, it is explained that after the exile of the tribes of Re'uven, Gad and half of Menashe, the Yovel was annulled. And the law of the shemita, which depends on the Yovel, will be explained in the next note. In the year 3,206, 18 years after the exile of Re'uven and Gad, Shalmaneser king of Assyria exiled the rest of the tribes that remained in the kingdom of Israel.<br>It is explained in Arakhin 33a that after about 116 years Yirmeyahu brought back the ten tribes, and Yoshiyahu son of Amon reigned over them, and the Yovel again applied by Torah law (and see Harḥavot on this). According to what is explained in Arakhin 12b; 32b, even after the ten tribes returned the count continued uninterrupted, for according to the Sages even after they were exiled they continued to count the Yovel for the sake of the shemitot. But according to Rabbi Yehuda, when the people of the ten tribes returned they began the count anew and counted 36 years until the destruction.<br>After the First Temple was destroyed, according to most of the Tanna'im and Amora'im the holiness of the Land for its mitzvot was annulled (Megilla 10a; Arakhin 32b), and so it was ruled in practice (Rambam, Terumot 1:5; Beit Ha-Beḥira 6:16). Therefore I wrote that during the seventy years of the Babylonian exile the shemitot were annulled. And so wrote the Rambam 10:3, and his source is in Arakhin 13a, that only from the time Ezra came up did they begin to count shemitot; this implies that until then they were not obliged to keep them even rabbinically. However, it is possible that although by law they were exempt from the mitzvot, they tried to keep Shevi'it as a custom, and this is somewhat implied from the Rambam 10:5, regarding the view of the Ge'onim. And so explained Rav Rabinovitch in the introduction to the laws of Shevi'it.<br>The count accepted in the period of the Rishonim is from the creation of Adam, which was on Rosh Ha-Shana (the molad of 'vayad', for so wrote the Seder Olam, Rashi and others). But afterwards they became accustomed to count from the creation of the world (the count 'le-yetzira', the molad of 'baharad'), that is, from the first day of creation, which is the twenty-fifth of Elul; and consequently one year is added to the count of years. And so we count today when we say that Shevi'it was in the year 5775, and so one writes in documents and in ketubot, and so we wrote above. (However, many write the dates of the Tanakh according to the Seder Olam, and so the count of years comes out one year earlier, unlike the way we count today.)"}]},{"slug":"16-05-04","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/16-05-04/","title_he":"ד – ימי בית המקדש השני","title_en":"4. The Days of the Second Temple","body_en":"<p>Seventy years after the destruction of the First Temple, in the year 3,410 by the count we use from the creation of the world, those who came up from Babylon began to build the Second Temple. Close to the beginning of its building Ezra the Scribe came up from Babylon, and together with his beit din sanctified the Land again for its mitzvot. Ezra's beit din are those called the Men of the Great Assembly; a hundred and twenty elders were members of this great beit din, among them the last of the prophets, Ḥaggai, Zekharya and Malakhi, and likewise Daniel, Ḥananya, Misha'el and Azarya, and Neḥemya son of Ḥakhalya, and Mordekhai Bilshan, and Zerubavel (the Rambam's introduction to the Mishneh Torah).</p>\n<p>However, the obligation of Shevi'it in the days of the Second Temple was rabbinic only, because we have already learned that the mitzva of Shevi'it depends on the Yovel, and the law of the Yovel depends on all the tribes living on their land, each tribe in its inheritance: when every person of Israel has an inheritance from which he can make a living, and even if he is forced to sell it because of his poverty, in the Yovel the land will return to him or to his heirs. And in the days of the Second Temple only some of Israel returned to the Land from their exile, and they were mainly of the tribes of Yehuda, Binyamin and Levi; consequently the twelve tribes were not living in their inheritance, and by Torah law they were not obligated in the mitzvot of Yovel and Shevi'it. But the Sages, that is, Ezra and his beit din, instituted counting shemitot and Yovelot and keeping the mitzva of Shevi'it by their authority. And so it continued throughout all the days of the Second Temple, which stood for four hundred and twenty years.<sup>3</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":3,"en":"Gittin 36a: \"Rabbi says: 'And this is the manner of the release: release' (Devarim 15:2) – Scripture speaks of two releases, one the release of land (in the Yovel) and one the release of money (in Shevi'it); at a time when you release land, you release money; at a time when you do not release land, you do not release money.\" And so it is brought in Mo'ed Katan 2b; and Kiddushin 38b. And indeed there is a view among the Tanna'im according to which Shevi'it does not depend on the Yovel (Torat Kohanim Behar, brought in Rashi, Gittin 36a). But it is explicit in the Yerushalmi that the halakha follows Rabbi (Shevi'it 10:2; Demai 2:1; Gittin 4:3). And so it emerges from the sugyot of the Bavli, that at a time when the Yovel is annulled Shevi'it too is annulled (Introduction to Shabbat Ha-Aretz 1-3). And so ruled the Rambam 10:9; Tosafot Arakhin 32b, s.v. 'Manu'; Semag, positive 148; Semak 259; Yere'im 164. And so wrote the Rashba, the Ritva and the Ran on Gittin 36b; Tashbetz 3:201; Mabit 2:64; Mahari\"t, Yoreh De'ah 52; Maharalbaḥ 143; Maharashdam, Nivḥar Mi-Kesef, Yoreh De'ah 23; Yeshu'ot Malko, Yoreh De'ah 56; Avnei Nezer, Yoreh De'ah 458. And so wrote the Baḥ, the Sma, the Mahariqash, the Knesset Ha-Gedola on Shulḥan Arukh, Ḥoshen Mishpat 67:1. And so wrote the Shemen Ha-Mor, Yoreh De'ah 4; Rav Pe'alim 1, Sod Yesharim 6:10; Aḥi'ezer 2, Yoreh De'ah 39; Maran Rav Kook in the Introduction to Shabbat Ha-Aretz.<br>And it is difficult, for we find (Arakhin 31b) that the law of buying a house in walled cities applied in the days of the Second Temple, and likewise the law of a Hebrew slave (Keritot 11a), and these laws apply only at a time when the Yovel applies. Rabbeinu Tam answered (Tosafot Gittin 36a) that indeed the Yovel applied by Torah law in the days of the Second Temple, because it is enough that some of every tribe are in their place for the Yovel to apply; and Yirmeyahu brought back some of them in the days of Yoshiyahu, and some of them returned with those who came up from Babylon. But as we have learned, the view of the great majority of the poskim is that the law of Shevi'it in the days of the Second Temple was rabbinic. Therefore the Ramban, the Ran and the Ritva explained (Gittin there) that only when the majority of the members of every tribe live in their inheritance does the Yovel apply, but rabbinically some of the laws of the Yovel also applied in the days of the Second Temple. And from the Rambam it is implied that the laws of the Yovel did not apply at all in the days of the Second Temple, for he wrote (10:3): \"Although there was no Yovel in the Second Temple, they would count it in order to sanctify the shemitot.\" However, it is possible that he too would agree that some of the laws of the Yovel applied.<br>The count of the shemitot and the Yovel in the days of the Second Temple: according to the Sages, after Ezra the Scribe and his company came up, in the seventh year of the building of the Second Temple, they began to count years for Shevi'it and for the Yovel (Arakhin 13a). In the thirteenth year they kept the first Shevi'it, and in the fifty-sixth year they sanctified a Yovel. And according to Rabbi Yehuda, they began the count from the first year of the building of the Temple (Arakhin 12b; and see below in note 6)."}]},{"slug":"16-05-05","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/16-05-05/","title_he":"ה – קשיי קיום השביעית והניסיון להתירה","title_en":"5. The Difficulties of Keeping Shevi'it and the Attempt to Annul It","body_en":"<p>After the destruction of the Second Temple there were years in which the situation of the Jews in the Land was very hard. In addition to the constant oppression of the Romans who ruled the Land and imposed high taxes on the Jews, at times years of drought or war came, in which the Romans demanded higher taxes to supply their armies. And when these years fell around the seventh year, the burden was unbearable. Therefore Rabbi Yehuda Ha-Nasi (Rabbi) wanted to annul Shevi'it, for since it is a rabbinic mitzva, the Sages can annul it in a pressing situation. But Rabbi Pinḥas ben Ya'ir, one of the righteous of the generation, did not agree to join in this, for he held that it was possible to survive in Shevi'it under pressure, and Rabbi withdrew his intention (Yerushalmi Demai 1:3; Ta'anit 3:1). And seemingly one must ask: how did Rabbi think to annul Shevi'it, when the rule is that a beit din cannot annul the words of a fellow beit din unless it is greater than it in wisdom and in number, and the beit din that instituted the keeping of Shevi'it was the beit din of Ezra the Scribe, than which none was greater, and it is the one called 'the Great Assembly'? Rather, Rabbi held that since the need for this was very pressing, and consequently many of Israel transgressed and worked in Shevi'it, to the point that they said: \"Israel became suspect regarding Shevi'it\" (Gittin 54a), Shevi'it could be annulled even by a smaller beit din. But since he did not receive full agreement for this from the sages of his generation, he could not prevail in establishing an important beit din that would annul Shevi'it.</p>\n<p>But in whatever he could, he was lenient, even contrary to the accepted rules of halakha. Therefore he permitted working in Beit She'an, Caesarea, Beit Guvrin and Kfar Tzemaḥ. And likewise he permitted vegetables at the end of Shevi'it (Yerushalmi Demai 2:1).</p>\n<p>And once they brought before Rabbi a person who had worked his field in Shevi'it so that he would punish him; but Rabbi refrained from punishing him and said: And what shall this poor man do? He works to keep himself alive (Yerushalmi Ta'anit 3:1). The Sages further said that fasts and prayers are held in a year of drought even when it falls in Shevi'it, for the livelihood of others. And they asked: who are the others for whom one fasts that rain will fall, when Israel must rest from the work of the field in Shevi'it? Some explained that they fasted for the gentiles, for if rain did not fall the grain would become expensive, and Israel too would suffer from this. And Rabbi Ze'ira explained that they fasted for suspect Jews who worked their fields to keep themselves alive; and they explained that his view is like the view of Rabbi, who was lenient towards the poor who do work in Shevi'it (Yerushalmi there).<sup>4</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":4,"en":"Since the obligation of Shevi'it is rabbinic, Rabbi sought to annul Shevi'it because of the pressure on the poor (Korban Ha-Eda and Penei Moshe), and because of the many suspects who in any case did not keep Shevi'it (Penei Moshe). And from their words it clearly emerges that he sought to annul Shevi'it entirely. And the Mahara Fulda explained that he wanted to permit \"specifically for what they need to live.\" And some explained that his wish to annul was in a year of drought (Rash Sirillo, the Gra). And according to the Ḥazon Ish, Shevi'it 22:4, Rabbi thought to permit because of danger to life, and Rabbi Pinḥas ben Ya'ir did not think there was danger to life in this, since it was possible to survive on chicory. Thus far. But Maran Rav Kook (in the Introduction to Shabbat Ha-Aretz, chapter 3) explained as the commentators did, that Rabbi wanted to annul Shevi'it entirely because of the pressure and for the sake of the suspects."}]},{"slug":"16-05-06","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/16-05-06/","title_he":"ו – חיוב שביעית לאחר חורבן בית המקדש השני","title_en":"6. The Obligation of Shevi'it After the Destruction of the Second Temple","body_en":"<p>As we have learned, after the Second Temple was destroyed, the obligation to keep Shevi'it continued rabbinically. This is because according to most of the Tanna'im and Amora'im, although the first sanctification, with which the Land of Israel was sanctified at the time of the conquest of those who came up from Egypt, was annulled after the destruction of the First Temple, the second sanctification, with which the Land was sanctified for its mitzvot in the days of those who came up from Babylon, continued even after the destruction of the Second Temple. The Rambam explained that the reason for this is that the first sanctification was made through conquest; consequently, when the Land was taken from the hands of Israel, the conquest was annulled and the Land was exempted from the mitzvot dependent on it. But when those who came up from Babylon came up to the Land, they did not sanctify the Land through conquest but through the ḥazaka (taking possession) they made in it by settling it, and the ḥazaka was not annulled by the destruction and the exile. The meaning of ḥazaka is a 'claim of right' that is not annulled by the devastation of the Land at the hands of strangers. That is, since the Lord gave the Land to Israel, and Israel had already lived in it for close to a thousand years, established a kingdom in it, built the First Temple, and the Shekhina rested in it, and even the kings of the nations recognised Israel's right to their Land, as stated in the declaration of Koresh, the conquest of the Land by strangers cannot annul our claim of right to the Land, and consequently its holiness as regards the mitzvot dependent on it is not annulled.</p>\n<p>However, we have already learned (in halakha 4) that only when all the tribes of Israel are living on their land in their proper places do the Yovel and Shevi'it apply by Torah law; and since those who came up from Babylon did not merit this, the obligation of Shevi'it in the days of the Second Temple was rabbinic only, and this obligation continued even after the destruction of the Temple.</p>\n<p>But close to three hundred years after the destruction of the Second Temple, the beit din that sanctified months on the testimony of witnesses, intercalated years and counted Yovelot ceased; and some Rishonim hold that from then the obligation to keep Shevi'it was annulled. For the law of Shevi'it depends on the Yovel, and when they stopped counting years and Yovelot the obligation to keep Shevi'it was entirely annulled, and only as a matter of piety did people continue to keep Shevi'it. And the view of the great majority of the Rishonim is that even after the cessation of the beit din, the obligation to keep Shevi'it rabbinically remained.<sup>5</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":5,"en":"Most of the Rishonim and Aḥaronim hold that shemita in our time is rabbinic, as explained above in note 3. And some say that there is no obligation to keep Shevi'it in our time, and only as a custom of piety did some practise it. This is the view of the Razah (Terumot 45:4), the Ra'avad, the Or Zaru'a in the name of the Rashbam, and the Nimukei Yosef. And so wrote the Meiri (Magen Avot 15), and he wrote: \"many of the Ge'onim and rabbis who are with us.\" And the Rashbash, siman 258, wrote: \"Many great ones hold that it does not apply even rabbinically,\" and he wrote that so hold also the Behag, Rabbi Yehuda Ha-Nasi al-Bartzeloni, and Rabbeinu Yehuda ben Yakar. And the basis of their words is that the law of Shevi'it depends entirely on the Yovel; consequently, since the cessation of the beit din that sanctified months, intercalated years and counted shemitot and Yovelot, in the days of Hillel the Second in the year 4119 (359 by the general count), the mitzva of Shevi'it too was annulled. And therefore we find that in the days of the first Amora'im they kept shemita, for the beit din that counted Yovelot had not yet ceased (and see the Introduction to Shabbat Ha-Aretz 11).<br>And some inferred from the Rambam that Shevi'it in our time is from the Torah; but they could argue so because they had a faulty reading in the laws of Shemita and Yovel 10:9, whereas according to the correct reading the Rambam wrote explicitly that Shevi'it in our time is rabbinic. And Rav Rabinovitch in his introduction explains that according to the Rambam the obligation of Shevi'it in our time is because of custom, which is a rabbinic obligation of a lower level, since they did not explicitly institute that it be practised. And from the words of the Ramban (Sefer Ha-Zekhut on the Rif, Gittin 36a) it is implied that shemita is from the Torah, but in Makkot 3b he wrote explicitly that Shevi'it is rabbinic. And so the Rosh, in Makkot 1:3, and the Rashbash understood his view. It follows that there is not even one Rishon who wrote clearly that shemita in our time is from the Torah. However, there were a few among the Aḥaronim who held that Shevi'it is from the Torah, and some wanted to infer so from the words of other Rishonim; and presumably they too relied in their words on the faulty reading in the Rambam (the Netziv in Meshiv Davar 2:56; and the Beit Ha-Levi 3:1 inclines to this). But their words were rejected by the great majority of the poskim (see the Introduction to Shabbat Ha-Aretz 1-9; Yabi'a Omer, vol. 10, Yoreh De'ah 37)."}]},{"slug":"16-05-07","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/16-05-07/","title_he":"ז – חשבון השנה השביעית לימינו","title_en":"7. Counting the Seventh Year in Our Days","body_en":"<p>The Second Temple was built in the year 3,410 by the count we use from the creation of the world, stood for 420 years, and was destroyed at the end of the seventh year (Arakhin 11-12; Ta'anit 29a).</p>\n<p>According to many, the destruction was in the 421st year of the Temple's existence, which is the year 3,830 from the creation of the world, that is, the first year of the shemita cycle; therefore the shemita year in our time was 5775 (Rabbeinu Tam, Ri, Ramban, Ran, Terumah, Beit Yosef and Rema, Ḥoshen Mishpat 67:1; Rambam). And some say that the Temple was destroyed in the 420th year of its existence, which is the year 3,829 from the creation of the world, and this is the first year of the shemita cycle; therefore the shemita year in our time was 5774 (Rashi, Tur).</p>\n<p>A further doubt arose. Some hold that the Yovel year is not part of the count of shemitot, so that after every seven shemitot one stops counting for a year, corresponding to the Yovel, and only after that does one return to seven more cycles of seven years; and so one must act even after the destruction of the Temple. According to their view, Shevi'it fell in the year 5772. But according to the Ge'onim, from the time the Second Temple was destroyed they stopped counting Yovelot, and so the count of the seventh years is continuous. And the Rambam wrote that his own view was that one must continue to count Yovelot as well, but since the tradition follows the view of the Ge'onim, so one should act in practice, \"for tradition and practice are great pillars in ruling, and it is fitting to rely on them\" (Rambam, Shemita and Yovel 10:4-6). And the Ra'avad remained of the view that in our time too one must count the Yovelot, and Shevi'it was in 5772.<sup>6</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":6,"en":"The Yovel year is the fiftieth year of the shemita cycle, but the Tanna'im disagreed whether it is included within the cycle of shemitot. According to Rabbi Yehuda, the Yovel year is included in the count of the shemita, so that the full cycle is 49 years, and the Yovel year is counted as the first year of the count of the next Shevi'it. And according to the Sages, the Yovel adds another year outside the count of the shemitot, so that the full cycle is 50 years (Rosh Ha-Shana 9a).<br>According to Rabbeinu Tam and the Ri, the halakha follows Rabbi Yehuda, and the count of the shemitot began from the year the Temple was established, as explained in Arakhin 12b according to the view of Rabbi Yehuda. And the destruction was in the 421st year of its building, 3,830 from the creation of the world (70 CE), and this year was the first of the shemita; it follows that Shevi'it in our time was in 5775. And so hold many Rishonim.<br>According to Rashi too (Avoda Zara 9b) the halakha follows Rabbi Yehuda, but they counted the shemita years from the seventh year of the establishment of the Second Temple. And the destruction was in the 420th year of its building, 3,829 from the creation of the world (69 CE), and this year was the first of the shemita; it follows that Shevi'it in our time was in 5774. And so hold the Rosh (Avoda Zara 1:7) and the Tur (Ḥoshen Mishpat 67).<br>The Rambam agrees with Rashi that the destruction was in the 420th year of its building, but in his view the halakha follows the Sages (as below), and they began counting from the seventh year of the building of the Temple, and the year of the destruction is called the year after the destruction, and it was the year after Shevi'it. It follows that in practice his view is like the view of Rabbeinu Tam, that the year 3,830 is the first year of the shemita.<br>According to the Rambam and the Ra'avad, Shemita and Yovel 10:4; 6, the halakha follows the Sages. And in their view, even after the Temple was destroyed one must continue to count Yovelot, that is, add another year to the count every seven shemitot. According to this it comes out that the last Yovel year was in 5765, and the shemita after it in 5772. But the view of the Ge'onim is that although the halakha follows the Sages that the Yovel is not in the count of the shemitot, after the destruction Yovelot are no longer counted; and by this their count coincides with the count of Rabbeinu Tam and his company. And since the custom and the tradition were in the hands of the Ge'onim, the Rambam in practice set aside his own view. And the Ra'avad remained of his view that even today one must count Yovelot, and he thought that perhaps the Ge'onim too agree with this.<br>It follows that we have three views on the count of the shemitot (Rashi – 5774; Ra'avad – 5772; Rabbeinu Tam and the Ge'onim – 5775). And although until the end of the period of the Rishonim the dispute was not yet decided, and the Tur held like Rashi, and the Ra'avad remained of his view, nevertheless over time the custom was accepted according to the words of Rabbeinu Tam and the Ge'onim; and so wrote the Beit Yosef and the Rema, Ḥoshen Mishpat 67:1. And so summarised Responsa Maharalbaḥ, siman 143. And so decided the Sefer Ḥaredim, introduction to the mitzvot dependent on the Land of Israel. And so wrote the Mabit 1:50, in response to one who challenged the count.<br>And although the custom was decided regarding Shevi'it, and there is nobody who keeps it in another year, still some ruled, because of the doubt about the years, not to say a blessing over the redemption of ma'aser sheni, which applies in years 1, 2, 4 and 5 of the shemita cycle; and so wrote Rav Elyashar (Sefer Ish Emunim 30:1, se'if katan 7). But in practice it is agreed to say the blessing, as wrote Responsa Avkat Rokhel (siman 4) and the Birkei Yosef (Yoreh De'ah 331:23). And still there were some who held that because of the doubt about the years it is right to separate, out of doubt, both ma'aser sheni and ma'aser ani. So wrote Responsa Radbaz (2:731); Halakhot Ketanot (2:143); Rav Elyashar (Simḥa La-Ish, Yoreh De'ah 26), and that so his teacher, the author of Sefer Benei Binyamin, practised; the luaḥ dinim of the sages of Jerusalem from the year 5269 (letter 69). However, in the Avkat Rokhel (siman 4) and the Birkei Yosef (331:23) they did not write that one should make a condition. And so it is practised. And in Responsa Mateh Yosef (Yoreh De'ah 9) he wrote that the custom of making a condition is an ignorant custom against the custom of Israel. And these words were brought in the Ḥazon Ovadia (Terumot u-Ma'asrot p. 191, note 5), but he summarised in practice that those who wish to be stringent and make a condition may do so."}]},{"slug":"16-05-08","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/16-05-08/","title_he":"ח – גבולות ארץ ישראל – עולי מצרים ובבל","title_en":"8. The Borders of the Land of Israel: Those Who Came Up from Egypt and from Babylon","body_en":"<p>When Ezra and his beit din sanctified the Land for its mitzvot, he sanctified the place where those who came up from Babylon settled, and the place of their settlement was a smaller area than the area settled by those who came up from Egypt. But since the place of settlement of those who came up from Babylon was sanctified, the holiness was reawakened also in all the places conquered by those who came up from Egypt on both sides of the Jordan, and it even spread to Syria.</p>\n<p>All the kinds of field work forbidden in Shevi'it are forbidden in the whole area conquered by those who came up from Egypt, and also in Syria. But the decree on aftergrowths, by which the Sages decreed not to eat vegetables that grew by themselves in Shevi'it, applies only to the area sanctified by those who came up from Babylon (Rambam, Semag, Ḥinukh and others). And some are lenient and hold that within the borders of those who came up from Egypt and Syria it is even permitted to eat the fruit of Shevi'it after the time of removal (Rash). And some say that their fruit also has no holiness of Shevi'it (Gra).<sup>7</sup></p>\n<p>Plainly, every place under the sovereignty of the State of Israel is obligated in the mitzvot dependent on the Land, at the level of the borders of those who came up from Babylon. As the Rambam wrote (Laws of Terumot 1:2): \"The Land of Israel mentioned everywhere is the lands that a king of Israel, or a judge, or a prophet conquers with the consent of the majority of Israel, and this is what is called a conquest of the many.\" And Maran Rav Kook already explained that the Jewish public and its elected representatives have the status of a kingdom (Mishpat Kohen 144), and therefore what was conquered by the State of Israel is considered a conquest of the many. However, some have doubted this, either because perhaps the public and its representatives do not have the status of a kingdom, or because perhaps the Sanhedrin must sanctify the conquered place, or because perhaps the centre of the Land must be conquered first. And it seems more correct that all the places under Israeli sovereignty have the law of those who came up from Babylon.</p>\n<p>In practice, regarding the Negev and the Arava, there is not much need to be exact about the lines of the borders of those who came up from Babylon and those who came up from Egypt, since according to most of the poskim there is in any case no significant difference between them, for all the prohibitions of Shevi'it except the decree on aftergrowths apply also within the area of those who came up from Egypt. And even if we say that certain areas are not considered within the borders of those who came up from Egypt, it is then reasonable that Israeli sovereignty creates an obligation in all matters of the mitzvot dependent on the Land. All the more so is this the law regarding the Golan, since according to most of the poskim it is within the area of those who came up from Babylon. Therefore one must be careful with all the laws of Shevi'it within all the borders of the State of Israel.<sup>8</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":7,"en":"Mishna Shevi'it 6:1: \"Whatever those who came up from Babylon took possession of… is not eaten and not worked. And whatever those who came up from Egypt took possession of… is eaten but not worked.\" According to the Rambam 4:26, what they said \"is not eaten\" means that the Sages decreed there regarding the aftergrowths that they should not be eaten (as explained above 3:4), and within the borders of those who came up from Egypt they did not decree, and the aftergrowths are permitted to be eaten. And so is the view of most of the poskim, among them: the Semag, the Ḥinukh, Kaftor Va-Feraḥ, Tiferet Yisrael, Nivḥar Mi-Kesef, the Ḥazon Ish, Mishpetei Eretz 17:4; Ma'amar Mordechai 2:7. And some say that in addition to there being no decree on aftergrowths within the borders of those who came up from Egypt, the law of removal also does not apply to the fruit that grows there (the Rash and the Ramban according to Maran Rav Kook, Shabbat Ha-Aretz 4:26, 16). And some say that the law of the holiness of Shevi'it also does not apply to the fruit within the borders of those who came up from Egypt, but there is only a prohibition of work there (Mahari\"t, the Gra, Pe'at Ha-Shulḥan).<br>The law of Syria is like the law of the borders of those who came up from Egypt; and although in the time of the First Temple the obligation of the mitzvot within the borders of those who came up from Egypt was from the Torah and in Syria rabbinic, in the time of the Second Temple the obligation in both was rabbinic. And all the prohibitions of work in the field apply in Syria as in other places, and only in one matter is there a difference: the Sages instituted that one change the manner of processing the fruit of Shevi'it, doing it in small vessels or with another change, so that people will remember that it is fruit of Shevi'it, as explained above 3:3; and they did not institute this for fruit in Syria, as they said in Mishna Shevi'it 6:2: \"One may work with what is detached in Syria but not with what is attached; one may thresh, winnow, tread and bind sheaves, but not reap, harvest grapes or harvest olives.\" This is the view of the Rambam. And according to the Rash, even within the borders of those who came up from Egypt one need not change the manner of processing the fruit.<br>The Mor U-Ketzi'a (Oraḥ Ḥayim 306) wrote that the law the Sages fixed regarding the borders of those who came up from Babylon expanded to all the areas conquered by the Ḥashmonean kings, including Syria. But after they were taken from our hands, the law returned to apply only to the area that was properly settled by Jews in the time of the Second Temple, which is the borders of those who came up from Babylon, and its holiness remained for ever (and see Tevu'ot Ha-Aretz p. 21; Ma'adanei Eretz, Shevi'it 8:3). There is likewise a discussion whether most of the eastern side of the Jordan is considered within the borders of those who came up from Babylon (Semag, positive 161, in the name of Rabbeinu Tam; Tashbetz 3:198), or only within the borders of those who came up from Egypt (Ra'avad, Shemita and Yovel 4:28)."},{"n":8,"en":"Regarding the areas of the southern Arava as far as Eilat, many hold that the borders of those who came up from Egypt did not reach there (Or Le-Tziyon, Shevi'it 6:1; Yalkut Yosef, Shevi'it ve-Hilkhoteha 1:5; Shevet Ha-Levi 5:173). And some say that the borders of those who came up from Egypt explained in parashat Mas'ei include up to Eilat (Rav Sa'adia Gaon, Rav Tykocinski, Sefer Eretz Yisrael 26:2; Ma'amar Mordechai 2:9, 23; Ketif Shevi'it 4:1, 2; and see Otzar Eretz Yisrael by Rav Yisrael Ariel, part 5, chapter 30). And some say that in the time of Shlomo the kingdom extended as far as Eilat and the whole area was sanctified with the holiness of those who came up from Egypt (Tzitz Eliezer 3:23; Rav Goren in 'Mishnat Ha-Medina' p. 31; and see Mishmeret Ha-Shevi'it in the booklet at its end).<br>Regarding the status of the sovereignty of the State of Israel: some hold that it does not change the law (Eretz Ḥemda, vol. 1, Sha'ar 3, 6:3; 13:2; and Be-Ohalah Shel Torah, vol. 3, 1:4, inclines to this. And so it is implied from the position of the Ḥaredi rabbis, as Rav Kalman Kahana wrote at the end of 'Ha-Shevi'it Tishmetena'). And some say that the conquest and Israeli sovereignty obligate in the mitzvot like the law of those who came up from Babylon (Tzitz Eliezer 10:1; Rav Gershuni, Or Ha-Mizraḥ 22 p. 159; Rav Goren, 'Emunat Itekha' 50 pp. 49-63; Rav Aryeh Stern, 'Emunat Itekha' 50 pp. 70-71; Rav Weitman, Shemita Mamlakhtit chapter 11, 6; Rav Zoldan, 'Malkhut Yehuda ve-Yisrael' siman 24; Rav Neḥemya Rata, Or Ha-Ner 2 pp. 32-33). And so it is reasonable, all the more so since the whole Negev as far as Eilat is within the area of Shlomo's kingdom, and perhaps even within the borders of parashat Mas'ei.<br>All the more so regarding the Golan, which according to many is within the area of those who came up from Babylon (Tzitz Eliezer 10:1; Rav Yisraeli as brought in 'Ha-Torah ve-Ha-Aretz' vol. 3 p. 194; Rav Yigal Ariel in 'Eretz Yarden ve-Ḥermonim' p. 186).<br>However, in a pressing situation one may join the lenient views, which hold that there is no obligation of removal and no holiness of Shevi'it within the borders of those who came up from Egypt (but only a prohibition of work). And indeed regarding the decree on aftergrowths there is more room to be lenient, but this has no significance today, for nobody takes the trouble to pick plants that grew by themselves. So in practice this leniency regarding the borders of those who came up from Egypt has no practical significance. And likewise in a pressing situation one may join the lenient views that hold that the southern Negev is not within the borders of those who came up from Egypt, and that Israeli sovereignty does not create an obligation, so that it is permitted to work there. But the ordinary ruling is that all the laws of Shevi'it apply within all the borders of the State of Israel like the law of those who came up from Babylon. Therefore there is no need to map out the different views as to where the border of those who came up from Babylon runs in the Negev and the Arava."}]},{"slug":"16-05-09","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/16-05-09/","title_he":"ט – אמירה לגוי לעבוד בקרקע ישראל","title_en":"9. Telling a Non-Jew to Work Land of Israel","body_en":"<p>As is known, although a non-Jew may do work on Shabbat, the Sages forbade a Jew to ask a non-Jew to do work for him on Shabbat, and this prohibition is called the prohibition of 'shevut' or 'amira le-goy' (telling a non-Jew). Included in this, they also forbade telling a non-Jew to do work that is forbidden rabbinically on Shabbat, and this prohibition is called 'shevut de-shevut'. And only for a great need or for the sake of a mitzva did they permit 'shevut de-shevut' (Peninei Halakha, Shabbat 25:1; 9:11-12; 25:4). And the same law applies to the other Torah prohibitions whose punishment is death: it is forbidden to ask a non-Jew to do them, and it is even forbidden to ask a non-Jew to do the rabbinic prohibitions that were established as a fence for them, and this prohibition too is called 'shevut de-shevut'. And only for a great need or for the sake of a mitzva did they permit 'shevut de-shevut'.</p>\n<p>But a doubt arose regarding Torah prohibitions of lesser severity, which carry no death penalty: whether they too carry a prohibition of 'shevut' (Bava Metzia 90a). In practice, although some permit, the view of most of the poskim is that with the other Torah prohibitions too, telling a non-Jew is forbidden rabbinically (Rambam and Rosh). But when the prohibition is rabbinic, it is agreed that it is permitted to tell a non-Jew to do it, for there is no prohibition of 'shevut de-shevut' with Torah prohibitions that carry no death penalty.</p>\n<p>Therefore, since the Torah did not set a death penalty for one who transgresses Shevi'it, even when the mitzva of Shevi'it was from the Torah it was permitted to ask a non-Jew to do in Shevi'it work whose prohibition is rabbinic. And today, when Shevi'it is rabbinic, by the basic law it is permitted to ask a non-Jew to do any work.</p>\n<p>However, some hold that the mitzva of Shevi'it is that the land itself rest, and that included in the mitzva is preventing a non-Jew from working in our field, because by this he violates the rest; all the more so it is forbidden to tell him to do work in the field (Minḥat Ḥinukh 112:2). But the view of the great majority of the poskim is that the mitzva of Shevi'it is that Israel rest, and there is no mitzva that the land rest; therefore a Jew may in Shevi'it ask a non-Jew to do for him work whose prohibition is rabbinic. And so is the halakha. And in practice, although today the mitzva of Shevi'it is rabbinic, the practice is to be lenient with work whose basis is from the Torah only in pressing cases, and with work whose basis is rabbinic even not in a pressing situation.</p>\n<p>Therefore a road contractor who must plant non-fruit bushes at the sides of the road to prevent erosion may hire a non-Jew in Shevi'it to prepare the ground and plant the bushes (above 2:10). And likewise a building contractor who must prepare the ground around the buildings for a garden may hire a non-Jew to level the ground and spread garden soil over it (above 2:7). And from the outset it is right to create such a deal that the non-Jew too will be a partner in its profits, for then he does his work for himself.<sup>9</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":9,"en":"The Gemara, Bava Metzia 90a, did not resolve the doubt whether with the other Torah prohibitions that carry no death penalty the Sages also forbade telling a non-Jew to do the forbidden thing for the Jew's need. Some say that since the prohibition of telling a non-Jew is rabbinic, the law is that one is lenient in the doubt raised in the Gemara (brought in the Rosh and the Meiri). And the view of the great majority of the poskim is to be stringent. And so wrote the Rambam and the Rosh. And so it was ruled in the Beit Yosef and the Rema, Yoreh De'ah 297:4 (the second). And likewise in the Shulḥan Arukh, Even Ha-Ezer 5:14; Ḥoshen Mishpat 338:6.<br>But the poskim disagreed about a situation in which the non-Jew does the thing for his own need, for example when he earns a percentage from it. Some say that it is permitted, since he is not doing it only for the Jew; and so hold: the Rema, Yoreh De'ah 297:4 (the second), following the Haggahot Mordechai, Haggahot Asheri and the Maggid Mishneh. And on the other hand the Shakh there, 4, wrote that many forbid, namely: Rashi, the Ri, the Rosh, the Agudah and the Nimukei Yosef, and so it is implied from the Rambam; see there. (In Shabbat Ha-Aretz, Kuntres Aḥaron 1, end of se'if katan 1, he explained that the reason for the prohibition according to their view is that it appears that the non-Jew is doing it as the Jew's agent; see there.)<br>With Torah prohibitions that carry no death penalty there is no prohibition of 'shevut de-shevut'; that is, it is permitted to tell a non-Jew to do work that is forbidden rabbinically as a fence for a Torah prohibition that carries no death penalty. And so wrote the Maharam of Rothenburg (Prague 559); the Roke'aḥ as brought in the Peri Ḥadash, Oraḥ Ḥayim 468:1; Birkei Yosef 551:4; Peri Megadim, Mishbetzot Zahav 307:4; Erekh Ha-Shulḥan 696:1; Ḥazon Ovadia, Shabbat, vol. 3, pp. 492-495. According to this, even when Shevi'it was from the Torah, it was permitted to ask a non-Jew to do work whose prohibition is rabbinic; and today, when Shevi'it is entirely rabbinic, it is permitted to ask a non-Jew to do any work.<br>However, some learned from Avoda Zara 15b that the mitzva is that the land rest, and therefore a Jew may not let a non-Jew work in his field. So wrote Tosafot Rabbeinu Elḥanan there, the Maharshal (Bava Metzia 90a), and the Minḥat Ḥinukh (112:2). But from the words of Tosafot, Avoda Zara there, s.v. 'Mi', it emerges that the prohibition is that a Jew work, but there is no mitzva that the land rest. And so it is implied from the Ḥinukh 112; Beit Yitzḥak, Yoreh De'ah, vol. 2, 121:8-9. And so wrote the Pe'at Ha-Shulḥan 23:31; Maran Rav Kook in Shabbat Ha-Aretz 1:1; Kuntres Aḥaron 1. And so it is implied from all the other Rishonim and Aḥaronim, who did not mention that there is a prohibition for a non-Jew to work the land, as the Aderet wrote (brought at the end of Shabbat Ha-Aretz), that no Tanna or Amora, Rishon or Aḥaron, mentioned this. And so wrote Rav Shlomo Zalman Auerbach (Ma'adanei Eretz, Shevi'it 13), and the Binyan Av 3:41. And since the view of the great majority of the poskim is to be lenient, and also Shevi'it in our time is rabbinic, it is clear and obvious that there is no need at all to take into account the view of those who are stringent.<br>When a Jew asks a non-Jew to sow for him in his field, what grows in the Jew's field is permitted to be eaten and the decree on aftergrowths does not apply to it, because the decree is a fence against transgressors, but when a non-Jew does it there is no prohibition in the matter (Rambam in Responsa Pe'er Ha-Dor 15; Maran Rav Kook, Shabbat Ha-Aretz, Kuntres Aḥaron 1:1)."}]},{"slug":"16-05-10","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/16-05-10/","title_he":"י – אמירה לגוי לעבוד בשדה של הגוי","title_en":"10. Telling a Non-Jew to Work the Non-Jew's Field","body_en":"<p>It is permitted from the outset to rent agricultural tools to a non-Jew so that he may work with them in a field that he owns in the Land of Israel, and there is no prohibition of assisting a transgression in this, since the non-Jew is not commanded to rest in Shevi'it. In the past, when people ploughed with animals, a Jew was permitted to rent his animal to a non-Jew for ploughing in Shevi'it, for only on Shabbat is a Jew commanded that his animal also rest; but in Shevi'it there is no mitzva that the animal rest (Avoda Zara 15b).</p>\n<p>And likewise it is permitted to say to a non-Jew in Shevi'it: Plough your field in order to improve it, for at the end of Shevi'it I want to lease it from you. Because a non-Jew has no prohibition against working his own field in Shevi'it, and he ploughs mainly for himself, so that he can earn money by leasing it to a Jew (Mishna Shevi'it 4:3).</p>\n<p>And likewise it is permitted to give a non-Jew ploughing tools and wheat so that the non-Jew will sow it in his field in Shevi'it, on condition that he shares the crop with the Jew. For even at a time when Shevi'it was from the Torah, the prohibition of telling a non-Jew concerns land of a Jew; but when the land belongs to a non-Jew there is no prohibition.<sup>10</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":10,"en":"Mishna Shevi'it 4:3: \"One may lease ploughed fields from non-Jews in Shevi'it, but not from Jews.\" The view of most of the commentators is that the meaning is to agree with the non-Jew in Shevi'it to receive from him after Shevi'it his ploughed field on lease, even though by this one causes the non-Jew to plough his field in Shevi'it. And so wrote the Rivmatz, the Rash, the Ra'av, Tiferet Yisrael. And so it is explained in the Tosefta 3:7. However, in the Mishna there they also said: \"One may strengthen the hands of non-Jews in Shevi'it but not the hands of Jews.\" And in the Yerushalmi there two explanations were brought: (a) that the meaning is to agree with him that he will plough so that the Jew will lease the field from him after Shevi'it; (b) to encourage him in his work. And in the Bavli, Gittin 62a, only the second explanation is brought. And some hold, according to this, that only encouraging him is permitted, but it is forbidden to agree with him that he will plough his field in Shevi'it in order to lease it to a Jew after Shevi'it (some learned this from the Rambam 8:8 and other commentators). But the view of most of the Rishonim is that the discussion is about the explanation of the Mishna, but it is agreed in practice that it is permitted to agree with a non-Jew that he will plough his field in Shevi'it so that a Jew will lease the field from him after Shevi'it. And so wrote in practice the Rosh, the Semag and the Pe'at Ha-Shulḥan 28, se'if katan 25; and this is because the non-Jew ploughs for his own profit. And since most of the poskim are lenient, and it is also a rabbinic law, for the whole prohibition of telling a non-Jew is rabbinic only, the halakha follows the lenient. All the more so since some hold that there is no prohibition of telling a non-Jew with other prohibitions, and all the more so since Shevi'it in our time is rabbinic, and there is no prohibition at all of telling a non-Jew with a rabbinic prohibition, as brought in the previous note. (And even if we accept the learning of the stringent from the Mishna, that it is forbidden to ask a non-Jew to work his field in Shevi'it, one can say that the Mishna spoke of a situation in which Shevi'it is from the Torah and telling a non-Jew is forbidden in Shevi'it; but in our times even they would agree that telling a non-Jew is permitted.)<br>And so ruled in practice the Maharitatz (Yeshanot 47); Neḥpa Ba-Kesef, vol. 1, Yoreh De'ah 4; Peri Ha-Aretz 3:4, that it is permitted to give a gentile wheat so that he will sow it in his field for a Jew. And today some of the Ḥaredim agree with non-Jews that they will grow vegetables for them in Shevi'it, and argue against them, on the basis of what they learned from the Rambam and other commentators, that it is forbidden to tell a non-Jew to work his field in Shevi'it. And although they are mistaken and harm the mitzva of settling the Land, which is equal to all the mitzvot, and transgress \"buy from the hand of your neighbour\" (Vayikra 25:14, Sifra there) by preferring gentiles over members of their own people who act according to the heter mekhira (as explained below 9:1), nevertheless, as explained above, it is permitted to tell a non-Jew to work his field for a Jew. And see Shevitat Ha-Sadeh 3:4, 10.<br>It should be noted: the land belongs to the non-Jew only if it was actually bought from a Jew or received from the government according to the law; but lands that the non-Jew or his fathers seized by force are considered stolen, and their theft does not annul the ownership of the Jew from whom it was stolen. In practice, many lands today are considered state lands, and Arabs took them over illegally, and their law is not like land of a non-Jew. And some say further that even what various kingdoms conquered in the Land does not annul the ownership of the Jew. However, plainly according to most of the poskim 'the law of the kingdom is law' also in the Land of Israel (and see Ma'amar Mordechai siman 21, and the article of Rav Amiḥai, Ketif Shevi'it pp. 359-361)."}]},{"slug":"16-05-11","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/16-05-11/","title_he":"יא – דין פירות הגדלים בשדה של גוי","title_en":"11. The Law of Fruit Growing in a Non-Jew's Field","body_en":"<p>The poskim disagree about the law of fruit that grew in Shevi'it in a non-Jew's field. According to the Mabit, it is holy with the holiness of Shevi'it, and therefore it is forbidden to destroy it; but since it grew in a non-Jew's land, it is permitted to trade in it. And if a Jew buys it and gathers it from the field, it will be exempt from terumot and ma'asrot, like the law of fruit of Shevi'it, which is exempt from terumot and ma'asrot.</p>\n<p>By contrast, the custom in the Land of Israel was that fruit that grew in the fields of a non-Jew has no holiness of Shevi'it at all, for the non-Jew's ownership removes it from its mitzvot. And since the law of Shevi'it does not apply to it, if a Jew buys the fruit and gathers it from the field, since the obligation of terumot and ma'asrot depends on the work of gathering it, and that was done by a Jew, he will be obliged to separate terumot and ma'asrot from it. And Rabbi Yosef Karo strengthened this custom in his responsa. And since the sages of Tzfat wanted to preserve the custom and prevent a dispute from developing, they decreed excommunication on anyone who would treat fruit of Shevi'it that grew in the fields of non-Jews as holy and not separate terumot and ma'asrot from it.</p>\n<p>However, a doubt arose whether one should separate ma'aser sheni or ma'aser ani from it: for in years 1, 2, 4 and 5 of the shemita cycle one separates ma'aser sheni, and in years 3 and 6 ma'aser ani; and the question is what the law is with fruit that grew in the fields of a non-Jew in Shevi'it and was gathered by Jews. According to Rabbi Yosef Karo and most of the poskim, one separates ma'aser ani from it, as a continuation of the previous year in which ma'aser ani was separated (Rema, Yoreh De'ah 331:19). And some were concerned to separate ma'aser sheni as well out of doubt, and Maran Rav Kook wrote that this is fitting from the outset. However, the practice is not to say a blessing over the separation of terumot and ma'asrot, out of consideration also for the view of the Mabit.<sup>11</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":11,"en":"The Tanna'im disagreed (Yerushalmi Demai 5:8) whether a non-Jew has ownership in the Land of Israel that removes the mitzvot dependent on the Land. According to Rabbi Meir there is no ownership, that is, his purchase of the land does not remove it from the mitzvot; and according to Rabbi Yehuda and Rabbi Shimon there is ownership, so that his ownership removes it from the mitzvot. And likewise in the Bavli (Gittin 47a) Amora'im disagreed about this: according to Rabba there is no ownership, and according to Rabbi Elazar there is ownership. In the Yerushalmi it was ruled explicitly according to Rabbi Yehuda and Rabbi Shimon, that a non-Jew has ownership to remove the land from the obligation of the mitzvot. And so wrote the Ra'av (Pe'ah 4:9; Demai 5:9). And so is the view of Rabbeinu Ḥananel and the Arukh, as brought at the beginning of the next note. But in the Bavli it is somewhat implied that there is no ownership, and so is the view of most of the poskim. And so wrote the Rambam in the Laws of Terumot 1:10: \"A non-Jew who bought land in the Land of Israel did not remove it from the mitzvot, but it remains in its holiness. Therefore, if a Jew went back and took it from him, it is not like an individual's conquest, but he separates terumot and ma'asrot…\" The Mabit explained that even while the land is in the hands of the non-Jew it is obligated in the mitzvot. Whereas according to Rabbi Yosef Karo, when the land is in the hands of the non-Jew it is removed from the obligation of the mitzvot, and only after the Jew buys it does it again become obligated; and this is the meaning of the halakha that the non-Jew's ownership does not remove the land from the mitzvot. And so learned the Avkat Rokhel 24, specially regarding Shevi'it, from the exposition of the Sages (Sifra Behar, parasha 1): \"'For you' – and not for others\": that even if we say that a non-Jew has no ownership in the Land of Israel for the other mitzvot, the law of Shevi'it is special, applying to land that is yours and not when it is in the hands of non-Jews. And so wrote the Pe'at Ha-Shulḥan 23:29.<br>In practice, the view of Rash Sirillo is that all the mitzvot of Shevi'it apply to fruit growing in land of a non-Jew. And so holds the Mabit, but in his view, although it has the holiness of Shevi'it, it has no prohibition of trade and perhaps also no obligation of removal; and according to his son the Mahari\"t (1:43), it has no prohibition of trade but is obligated in removal. And so wrote the Sefer Ḥaredim chapter 56; the Shelah; Sha'arei Tzedek 16:14; Ḥazon Ish 20:7. And Maran Rav Kook wrote (Shabbat Ha-Aretz, Kuntres Aḥaron 20) that even according to those who hold that a non-Jew has no ownership, the meaning is that rabbinically the mitzva applies, but by Torah law the fruit growing in a non-Jew's field is exempt from the mitzvot dependent on the Land.<br>But the main view, according to the long-standing custom in the Land of Israel, and as the sages of Tzfat with Rabbi Yosef Karo ruled, is that there is no holiness of Shevi'it in fruit that grew in a non-Jew's field, and if a Jew gathered it he must separate terumot and ma'asrot from it; and they even excommunicated anyone who acted otherwise. And so wrote the Tur (Laws of the Land of Israel 2), the Rema (Yoreh De'ah 331:19), the Mahariqash, the Derisha, the Maharitatz (Yeshanot 98); Birkei Yosef 331:10. And so wrote the Pe'at Ha-Shulḥan 23:29; Maran Rav Kook (Shabbat Ha-Aretz 4:29); Yabi'a Omer, vol. 10, Yoreh De'ah 42. And so hold all those who agree to the heter mekhira (below 7, note 3). And since the obligation of terumot and ma'asrot applies at the time the fruit is gathered (smoothing of the pile), if the non-Jew sold his fruit to a Jew who gathers it from the field, he is obliged to separate terumot and ma'asrot from it, for the law of Shevi'it does not apply to it, since while it was in the hands of the non-Jew he removed it from the mitzvot of Shevi'it. And see Ketif Shevi'it 70:10-15."}]},{"slug":"16-05-12","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/16-05-12/","title_he":"יב – האם מותר ליהודי לעבוד בשדה של גוי","title_en":"12. May a Jew Work in a Non-Jew's Field?","body_en":"<p>Some say that a Jew may not work in Shevi'it on land belonging to a non-Jew, because the halakha follows the view of those who hold \"a non-Jew has no ownership in the Land of Israel\" (Mabit). And even according to those who hold that fruit that grew in the fields of non-Jews has no holiness of Shevi'it, this is because the power of the non-Jew's ownership can remove the holiness of the fruit, but it does not have the power to remove the holiness of the land itself; therefore a Jew may not work it (Ḥazon Ish).</p>\n<p>And some say that a Jew may work in Shevi'it on land belonging to a non-Jew, and this permission includes even kinds of work whose prohibition is from the Torah, because \"a non-Jew has ownership in the Land of Israel\": if he bought land, the mitzvot dependent on the Land do not apply to it, including the mitzva of Shevi'it. And some say that indeed, at a time when the obligation of the mitzva of Shevi'it is from the Torah, the non-Jew's ownership does not remove the mitzva of Shevi'it, and a Jew may not work on land belonging to a non-Jew; but when the mitzva of Shevi'it applies rabbinically only, the non-Jew's ownership has the power to remove the mitzva, and a Jew may work in a non-Jew's field in Shevi'it.<sup>12</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":12,"en":"In Sanhedrin 26a the Sages found merit in a Jew who ploughed a field in Shevi'it, suggesting that perhaps he was an 'agiston'. And Rabbeinu Ḥananel and the Arukh, and likewise Rashi in his first explanation, explained that he is a hired worker on land of a non-Jew. And Tosafot there explained that this is according to the view that a non-Jew has ownership to remove the obligation of the mitzvot. And as explained in the previous note, Tanna'im, Amora'im and Rishonim disagreed about this. If so, according to this view, since the non-Jew's ownership removes the land from its mitzvot, a Jew may work on land belonging to a non-Jew even at a time when the obligation of the mitzva of Shevi'it is from the Torah.<br>However, in Gittin 62a Rav said that a Jew may not work on land of a non-Jew, and so the Rambam ruled (8:8). And they explained that in his view the prohibition is rabbinic, because of appearance, lest people think the land is a Jew's (Maharitatz, Yeshanot 47). And the Sages who found merit in the one who worked perhaps disagree with this, or perhaps they raised the possibility that it was known in his place that he was a hired worker, and therefore there was no prohibition of appearance in his work (see Shulḥan Arukh, Oraḥ Ḥayim 243:1-2). And according to this view one may say that even at a time when Shevi'it is from the Torah a Jew may work in a non-Jew's field, for they decreed against it only because of appearance, and now the decree has lapsed, as will be explained further.<br>And many hold that the permission for a Jew to work in a non-Jew's field (Sanhedrin 26a) is when Shevi'it is rabbinic. And Rav, who forbade a Jew to work in a non-Jew's field, held that in his time, while the beit din still sanctified months and counted years, Shevi'it was from the Torah (as the view brought in the Torat Kohanim). But today, when the great majority of the Rishonim agree that Shevi'it is not from the Torah, a Jew may work in a non-Jew's field (Introduction to Shabbat Ha-Aretz 11; Otzrot Yosef; Yabi'a Omer, vol. 10, 42:6).<br>Similarly the Sefer Ha-Teruma wrote (Laws of the Land of Israel, vol. 3, p. 590) that when the obligation of the mitzva is from the Torah, a non-Jew has no ownership to remove the land from the obligation of the mitzvot; but when the obligation of the mitzva is rabbinic, his ownership removes it from the mitzva, like the law of Syria, whose obligation was always rabbinic, and all agree that a non-Jew has ownership to remove it from the mitzvot (Gittin 47a). And so wrote the Mahari Korkos (brought in the Kesef Mishneh, Terumot 1:13). And so is the view of the Gra (Yoreh De'ah 331:28) and the Pe'at Ha-Shulḥan 23:29. And what the Rambam ruled (8:8), that even when Shevi'it is rabbinic a Jew may not work in a non-Jew's field, is because of appearance (Maharitatz, Yeshanot 47). And Maran Rav Kook explained that this decree was decreed at a time when most of the land was in the hands of Jews, but in the time of exile, when most of the land was in the hands of non-Jews, the decree lapsed, and it should not be renewed without a new enactment (Shabbat Ha-Aretz, Kuntres Aḥaron 20). And the great ones after him added that in any case, at a time when it is customary to use the 'heter mekhira', even though most of the land belongs to Jews, there is no room for the decree, for everyone knows that they are working in a field that was sold to a non-Jew, as brought in Kerem Tziyon 19, Gidulei Tziyon 4, and Yabi'a Omer 10:42:6.<br>On the other hand, some hold that a non-Jew has no ownership to remove the mitzvot even when Shevi'it is rabbinic, and therefore the fruit growing in his field is holy with the holiness of Shevi'it, and the mitzva of Shevi'it applies to it. And as we learned in the previous note, this is the view of Rash Sirillo, the Mabit, the Mahari\"t, the Ḥaredim, the Shelah and the Ḥazon Ish. And consequently it is clear that in their view a Jew may not work in a non-Jew's field. And what is brought (Sanhedrin 26a), that the Sages found merit in a Jew who worked in Shevi'it, that perhaps he was an agiston, means that he was in a situation of danger to life (Tosafot Gittin 62a, s.v. 'Ein'). And some say that even according to those who hold that the fruit growing in a non-Jew's field has no holiness, nevertheless holiness remains on the land, and a Jew may not work it; and so it is brought in the Beit Yitzḥak, the Ḥazon Ish and the Ma'adanei Eretz. And not only this, but some learned from the words of the Kesef Mishneh (4:29) that if a Jew sowed in a non-Jew's field, the prohibition of aftergrowths applies to the crops. And my teacher and master Rav Yisraeli wrote (Ḥavat Binyamin 1:9:12) that one can say this only when Shevi'it is from the Torah, but when it is rabbinic it is clear that there is no prohibition of aftergrowths. (See further at the end of note 10, that there is no decree on aftergrowths on what a non-Jew sows in a Jew's field.)"}]}]});
